    <emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/HR</emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Hour</rdfs:label>
    <schema:unitCode rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">HUR</schema:unitCode>
    <emmo:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">h</emmo:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Measure of time defined as 3600 seconds.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The hour (common symbol: h or hr) is a unit of measurement of time. In modern usage, an hour comprises 60 minutes, or 3,600 seconds. It is approximately 1/24 of a mean solar day. An hour in the Universal Coordinated Time (UTC) time standard can include a negative or positive leap second, and may therefore have a duration of 3,599 or 3,601 seconds for adjustment purposes. Although it is not a standard defined by the International System of Units, the hour is a unit accepted for use with SI, represented by the symbol h.

-- QUDT</rdfs:comment>
    <emmo:EMMO_70fe84ff_99b6_4206_a9fc_9a8931836d84 xml:lang="en">Measure of time defined as 3600 seconds.</emmo:EMMO_70fe84ff_99b6_4206_a9fc_9a8931836d84>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Hour</skos:prefLabel>
    <emmo:EMMO_fe015383_afb3_44a6_ae86_043628697aa2 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.H02866</emmo:EMMO_fe015383_afb3_44a6_ae86_043628697aa2>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/units/siacceptedspecialunits#"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">The hour (common symbol: h or hr) is a unit of measurement of time. In modern usage, an hour comprises 60 minutes, or 3,600 seconds. It is approximately 1/24 of a mean solar day. An hour in the Universal Coordinated Time (UTC) time standard can include a negative or positive leap second, and may therefore have a duration of 3,599 or 3,601 seconds for adjustment purposes. Although it is not a standard defined by the International System of Units, the hour is a unit accepted for use with SI, represented by the symbol h.

-- QUDT</emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
