  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/emmo#Tex">
    <emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Units_of_textile_measurement</emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/2.1/vocab/unit</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/units/unclassifiedunits#"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">`Tex is a unit of measure for the linear mass density of fibers and is defined as the mass in grams per 1000 meters. Tex is more likely to be used in Canada and Continental Europe, while denier remains more common in the United States and United Kingdom. The unit code is 'tex'. The most commonly used unit is actually the decitex, abbreviated dtex, which is the mass in grams per 10,000 meters. When measuring objects that consist of multiple fibers the term 'filament tex' is sometimes used, referring to the mass in grams per 1000 meters of a single filament. Tex is used for measuring fiber size in many products, including cigarette filters, optical cable, yarn, and fabric.`

-- QUDT</emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Tex</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="n6a0b4d77db1d4bb4a28be148935c0b69b3380"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_4007522b_7ab7_4855_acd2_e99e2a0690b6"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_868ae137_4d25_493e_b270_21ea3d94849e"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_8246541a_f1f6_4d03_8bd7_fc6b76d17375"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="n6a0b4d77db1d4bb4a28be148935c0b69b3379"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">tex</emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">`Tex is a unit of measure for the linear mass density of fibers and is defined as the mass in grams per 1000 meters. Tex is more likely to be used in Canada and Continental Europe, while denier remains more common in the United States and United Kingdom. The unit code is 'tex'. The most commonly used unit is actually the decitex, abbreviated dtex, which is the mass in grams per 10,000 meters. When measuring objects that consist of multiple fibers the term 'filament tex' is sometimes used, referring to the mass in grams per 1000 meters of a single filament. Tex is used for measuring fiber size in many products, including cigarette filters, optical cable, yarn, and fabric.`

-- QUDT</rdfs:comment>
