  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/emmo#AssayTon">
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_77e9dc31_5b19_463e_b000_44c6e79f98aa"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_15344998_4490_4b40_b242_c3d92e5cdc27"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="n6a0b4d77db1d4bb4a28be148935c0b69b70"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_868ae137_4d25_493e_b270_21ea3d94849e"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="n6a0b4d77db1d4bb4a28be148935c0b69b69"/>
    <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://www.assaying.org/assayton.htm</rdfs:seeAlso>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Assay Ton</skos:altLabel>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">AssayTon</rdfs:label>
    <emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">In the United States, a unit of mass, approximately 29.167  grams. The number of milligrams of precious metal in one assay ton of the ore being tested is equal to the number of troy ounces of pure precious metal in one 2000-pound ton of the ore. i.e. a bead is obtained that weights 3 milligrams, thus the precious metals in the bead would equal three troy ounces to each ton of ore with the understanding that this varies considerably in the real world as the amount of precious values in each ton of ore varies considerably.

-- QUDT</emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">AssayTon</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/units/otherunits#"/>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/2.1/vocab/unit</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/TON_Assay</emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc>
    <emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">AT</emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">In the United States, a unit of mass, approximately 29.167  grams. The number of milligrams of precious metal in one assay ton of the ore being tested is equal to the number of troy ounces of pure precious metal in one 2000-pound ton of the ore. i.e. a bead is obtained that weights 3 milligrams, thus the precious metals in the bead would equal three troy ounces to each ton of ore with the understanding that this varies considerably in the real world as the amount of precious values in each ton of ore varies considerably.

-- QUDT</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
