  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/emmo#Coulomb">
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_1abe0ea7_2378_402f_b950_89b0795d2f45"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_0266ec8c_bd13_4c9c_be77_607498793ed3"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_9e5f464a_658c_4b34_b80d_a5f8d5ec811a"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_ab79e92b_5377_454d_be06_d61b50db295a"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_e9ffc696_5228_4ff9_8a60_0f5e05e9931b"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="n6a0b4d77db1d4bb4a28be148935c0b69b4773"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/C</emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc>
    <emmo:EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f xml:lang="en">The SI unit of electric charge. One coulomb is the amount of charge accumulated in one second by a current of one ampere. Electricity is actually a flow of charged particles, such as electrons, protons, or ions. The charge on one of these particles is a whole-number multiple of the charge e on a single electron, and one coulomb represents a charge of approximately 6.241 506 x 1018 e. The coulomb is named for a French physicist, Charles-Augustin de Coulomb (1736-1806), who was the first to measure accurately the forces exerted between electric charges.

-- QUDT</emmo:EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">SI unit for electric charge.</rdfs:comment>
    <emmo:EMMO_6dd685dd_1895_46e4_b227_be9f7d643c25 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI">http://dbpedia.org/resource/Coulomb</emmo:EMMO_6dd685dd_1895_46e4_b227_be9f7d643c25>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Coulomb</rdfs:label>
