  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/emmo#Dyne">
    <emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/DYN</emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_8246541a_f1f6_4d03_8bd7_fc6b76d17375"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="n6a0b4d77db1d4bb4a28be148935c0b69b577"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="n6a0b4d77db1d4bb4a28be148935c0b69b578"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_53e825d9_1a09_483c_baa7_37501ebfbe1c"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_868ae137_4d25_493e_b270_21ea3d94849e"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">In physics, the dyne is a unit of force specified in the centimetre-gram-second (CGS) system of units. One dyne is equal to In physics, the dyne is a unit of force specified in the centimetre-gram-second (CGS) system of units. One dyne is equal to 10 micronewton. Equivalently, the dyne is defined as 'the force required to accelerate a mass of one gram at a rate of one centimetre per square second'. The dyne per centimetre is the unit traditionally used to measure surface tension.

-- QUDT</rdfs:comment>
    <emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">dyn</emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64>
    <emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">In physics, the dyne is a unit of force specified in the centimetre-gram-second (CGS) system of units. One dyne is equal to In physics, the dyne is a unit of force specified in the centimetre-gram-second (CGS) system of units. One dyne is equal to 10 micronewton. Equivalently, the dyne is defined as 'the force required to accelerate a mass of one gram at a rate of one centimetre per square second'. The dyne per centimetre is the unit traditionally used to measure surface tension.

-- QUDT</emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
