  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/emmo#ElectronVolt">
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_6795a4b8_ffd0_4588_a581_a9413fe49cac"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_f6070071_d054_4b17_9d2d_f446f7147d0f"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_1abe0ea7_2378_402f_b950_89b0795d2f45"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_0266ec8c_bd13_4c9c_be77_607498793ed3"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="n6a0b4d77db1d4bb4a28be148935c0b69b4317"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_9e5f464a_658c_4b34_b80d_a5f8d5ec811a"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="n6a0b4d77db1d4bb4a28be148935c0b69b4318"/>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/units/siacceptedspecialunits#"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">An electron volt (eV) is the energy that an electron gains when it travels through a potential of one volt. You can imagine that the electron starts at the negative plate of a parallel plate capacitor and accelerates to the positive plate, which is at one volt higher potential. Numerically 1 eV approximates 1.6x10⁻¹⁹ joules, where 1 joule is 6.2x10¹⁸ eV. For example, it would take 6.2x10²⁰ eV/sec to light a 100 watt light bulb.

-- QUDT</emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
    <emmo:EMMO_6dd685dd_1895_46e4_b227_be9f7d643c25 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://dbpedia.org/page/Electronvolt</emmo:EMMO_6dd685dd_1895_46e4_b227_be9f7d643c25>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The amount of energy gained (or lost) by the charge of a single electron moving across an electric potential difference of one volt.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">An electron volt (eV) is the energy that an electron gains when it travels through a potential of one volt. You can imagine that the electron starts at the negative plate of a parallel plate capacitor and accelerates to the positive plate, which is at one volt higher potential. Numerically 1 eV approximates 1.6x10⁻¹⁹ joules, where 1 joule is 6.2x10¹⁸ eV. For example, it would take 6.2x10²⁰ eV/sec to light a 100 watt light bulb.

-- QUDT</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">ElectronVolt</rdfs:label>
